      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess the relationship between IL-6 and PAI-1 polymorphisms and coronary artery disease (CAD) and to observe the interactions between these polymorphic variants and smoking in the CAD risk."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN AND METHOD",
          "content": "The study population consisted of 178 patients with angiographically documented CAD and 202 blood donors. The analyses of genetic polymorphisms were performed using the PCR-RFLP method."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The frequency of PAI-1 5G allele was higher in the entire CAD group than in control group (p=0.04, OR=1.35). Also the 5G allele carriers (4G5G+5G5G) were more frequent in patients than in controls (p=0.03, OR=1.93). The number of women carrying 5G allele was again significantly higher among patients (OR=10.95 p=0.0075). The IL-6 C allele frequency was higher only in the CAD male subgroup (p=0.035, OR=1.44). We found synergistic and cumulative effects between specific genotype patterns and smoking in determining the risk of CAD, especially between PAI-1(4G5G+5G5G)+IL-6(CC) and smoking (SIM=4.18 and p=0.0005, OR=9.20, respectively)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "There are synergistic and cumulative effects of 5G allele of PAI-1 polymorphism and C allele of IL-6 polymorphism with smoking in determining their associated risk with CAD."
        }
      ]},
